remstart
    THE GLOW
    another hidden thing, and a prototype. the routines are in fish_routines_glow.

    there is always one cell of the board that glows. nothing says why. every swap that is counted as a
    move, and makes a match, steps the glow one cell the way that the swap went: the way that the cursor
    carried its fish. the swap can be anywhere on the board. so every swap is two things at once: the
    match that it makes, and a step of the glow, somewhere else. a swap makes a match when either of its
    two fish is in a group that is big enough, whether the group pops, or the night holds it, or it
    sings on coral. a swap that makes no match does not step the glow at all.
    a "free move" and the special moves do not step it, the same as they leave the tide alone.
    the swap says which way it went when it is made, and the glow steps when the board is looked at:
    glowSwapPending and glowJudgeSwap. so a loop that the step closes pops on the next look.

    THE EDGE. the glow stops at the edge of the board: a step that would take it off does nothing. with
    the "seamless" card it goes around the edge to the other side, the way that the cursor does.

    THE START. where the glow starts comes from the seed of the game. see glowPlace.

    THE TRAIL. the glow leaves a trail behind it, of the cells that it was on, like a snake. the
    trail can be as long as the multiplier is: one cell at x1, and four at x4. while it is shorter
    than that, a step makes it one longer. once it is that long, a step takes its oldest cell away, so
    it moves along. when the multiplier goes down, the trail loses its oldest cells until it fits.
    stepGlow does that, on the next frame. GLOW_TRAIL_OVERRIDE sets the length by hand, for the debug
    panel.

    A LOOP. when the glow steps onto a cell of its own trail, the trail has closed into a loop: the cell
    that it stepped onto, every cell of the trail since, and the cell that the glow stepped from. every
    fish that the loop encloses pops, as one forced group, and scores like one. the fish of the loop
    itself do not pop. then the trail is gone, and the glow starts over from where it is. stepping back
    onto the cell that it just came from closes a loop that encloses nothing: nothing pops, and the
    trail is gone all the same.
    a cell is enclosed when it can not get to the edge of the board without crossing the loop, going
    from cell to cell the four ways. with the "seamless" card there is no edge to get to: the loop cuts
    the board into pieces, and every piece but the biggest is enclosed.
    the pop is a forced pop: see FORCED POPS in fish_headers. the diver is never popped by it.

    WHAT SHOWS. nothing is drawn over the board. every fish on the path, which is the cell that glows
    and the whole of its trail, faces the other way from the rest. frame_fish_fx adds GLOW_TURN# to
    its yaw. a fish turns around slowly, and the turn is the fish's own: a fish that is swapped onto
    the path comes on facing the way that it was, and turns around there, and one that is swapped off
    turns back. swapCellStacks, in fish_funcs, takes the turn along with the fish, for a swap and for a
    fall. a new fish that drops in comes in facing the usual way, so one that lands on the path turns.
    a fish that falls through the path turns as it passes, by where it is drawn: a fish falls through
    cells before it is in any of them. see glowWhereCell.
    the trail only shows like that, and when the music plays: on every note of a tune that is on a
    beat of it, and not a rest, a faint glimmer of light sets off from the root of the trail, its oldest
    end, and slides along it to the tip, which is the cell that glows, the one that the next move moves.
    it goes underneath the fish, leaving a few soft sparkles as it goes. its path ripples to either side,
    and how tightly is up to the note that sent it, so every tune ripples a little differently. up to
    GLOW_RUN_COUNT of them run at once. a beat of a quick tune is two of its notes, the same as the
    dance counts them: see BEAT_PULSE_STEPS. the glimmer is the ribbon of a quest, drawn by
    GlowTrailEffect.fx, and by the copies of it that end in 2 and 3, since one effect can only be
    loaded once and each glimmer has its own path. melody notes come to glowMusicBeat, in
    fish_routines_music.

    the "Glow" window of the debug panel (FISH_DEBUG) turns it off and on, steps it by hand, sets
    the length of the trail, sends a pulse, and can light the cells of the trail in plain squares.
remend

global GLOW_ON = 1 `0 turns the whole thing off, for the debug panel
global GLOW_X = -1 `the cell that glows, across and down. -1 until glowPlace has put it where the seed says.
global GLOW_Y = 0
global GLOW_TRAIL_CAP = 64 `the most cells that the trail can have, whatever the length is set to
global dim GLOW_TRAIL_X(64) `the cells of the trail, the newest first
global dim GLOW_TRAIL_Y(64)
global GLOW_TRAIL_N = 0 `how many cells the trail has now
global GLOW_TRAIL_OVERRIDE = -1 `the length of the trail, set by hand, or -1 for the usual length

global FORCE_GLOW = 100 `the forced group of the fish that a loop encloses. see FORCED POPS in fish_headers.

` what the last loop did, for the debug panel
global GLOW_LOOPS = 0 `how many loops have been closed in this game
global GLOW_LAST_RING = 0 `how many cells the last loop had
global GLOW_LAST_INSIDE = 0 `and how many fish it popped

` scratch, for working out what a loop encloses
global dim GLOW_WALL(400) `1 for every cell of the loop
global dim GLOW_PIECE(400) `which piece of the board every cell is in, or 0 for none yet
global dim GLOW_QUEUE(400)
global dim GLOW_PIECE_SIZE(400)
global dim GLOW_PIECE_EDGE(400) `1 for a piece that gets to the edge, so it is not enclosed

` what is drawn. the cell that glows is lit under its fish, and so is the trail, fainter the older it is.
global GLOW_HEAD_SPRITE = 0
global dim GLOW_TRAIL_SPRITES(64)
global GLOW_SHOW_SQUARES = 0 `1 lights the cell that glows and the trail in plain squares, for the debug panel
global GLOW_HEAD_ALPHA = 200
global GLOW_TRAIL_ALPHA = 70 `how bright the newest cell of the trail is. the oldest is a third of that.
global GLOW_TINT = 0 `setup_glow picks it
global ORDER_GLOW = 9 `on top of the coral and the zones of the quests, and underneath the cursor and the fish

` WHAT SHOWS. the fish that are turned around.
global GLOW_TURN_ON = 1 `1 when the fish on the path are turned around. the debug panel can say no.
global dim GLOW_TURN#(400) `how far around the fish in every cell is turned, from 0 to 1, which is half of a turn. it goes with the fish.
global dim GLOW_TURNING(400) `the cells that are turned at all, or are on the path, so that only those are looked at
global GLOW_TURNING_N = 0
global dim GLOW_LISTED(400) `1 for a cell that is on that list
global dim GLOW_WANT(400) `GLOW_STAMP for a cell that is on the path on this frame
global GLOW_STAMP = 0
global GLOW_TURN_EASE# = 0.006 `how much of the way it turns in a millisecond
global GLOW_WHERE = 0 `scratch, for glowWhereCell: the cell that a fish is drawn in, or -1 for none
global GLOW_LAST_MS = 0

` the glimmers that run along the trail, on the beats of the music
global GLOW_RUN_COUNT = 3 `how many can be running at once. each one has a sprite, and an effect of its own.
global dim GLOW_RUN_STARTS(3) `when each one set off, on the clock of the game, or 0 for one that is not running
global dim GLOW_RUN_SPRITES(3)
global dim GLOW_RUN_FXS(3)
global GLOW_RUN_NEXT = 0 `the one that the next note sets off. it takes over from the oldest.
global GLOW_RUN_LAST = 0 `when the last one set off
global GLOW_RUN_CELL_MS = 110 `how long it takes to get from one cell of the trail to the next
global GLOW_RUN_GAP_MS = 0 `a note that comes sooner than this after the last one set off sends none
global GLOW_RUN_LENGTH = 1600 `how long the glimmer is, in thousandths of a cell
global GLOW_RUN_ALPHA = 60 `how bright it is, from 0 to 255
global GLOW_RUN_WIDE = 40 `how wide it is, in hundredths of a cell, from the middle to the edge
global GLOW_RUN_TINT = 0 `its color. setup_glow picks it: pale, and a little toward the water.
global GLOW_RUN_X# = 0 `scratch, for glowPathAt: a spot on the path, in pixels
global GLOW_RUN_Y# = 0
global dim GLOW_RIB#(24) `scratch, for glowDrawRun: the spots of the ribbon, x and then y of each
global GLOW_RUN_OK = 0 `and 0 when the path jumps around the edge of the board right there, with "seamless"
global GLOW_SPARK_NEXT = 0 `when the glimmers leave their next sparkle, on the clock of the game
global GLOW_SPARK_EVERY = 160 `one is left every this many milliseconds, and up to as long again
global GLOW_SPARK_ALPHA = 55 `how bright a sparkle is, from 0 to 255
global dim GLOW_RUN_NOTES(3) `the note that sent each glimmer. it says how tightly its path ripples.
global GLOW_WAVE_AMP = 30 `how far the path ripples to either side, in hundredths of a cell
global GLOW_WAVE_BASE = 40 `how many ripples there are to a cell, in hundredths, for the lowest note
global GLOW_WAVE_STEP = 7 `and how many more for every note higher
global dim GLOW_RIB_BASE#(24) `scratch, for glowDrawRun: the spots of the ribbon before they ripple
global dim GLOW_RIB_AT(12) `and how far along the path each one is, in thousandths of a cell

` a swap that has been made, and has not been judged yet. see glowSwapPending.
global GLOW_PENDING = 0 `1 while there is one
global GLOW_PENDING_X = 0 `which way it went
global GLOW_PENDING_Y = 0
global GLOW_SWAP_VALID = 0 `1 when handle_combos has seen a group that it put together
